{%- set churchIntro -%}<p class="page-intro">St Egwin's on Evesham Road is the parish church of Norton, the next village to Harvington. Its name honours St Egwin, bishop of Worcester and the founder of Evesham Abbey in the early 8th century. Norton and Lenchwick belongs to the Evesham benefice, but St Egwin's shares its Sunday rota with Harvington and the other ARCH churches. It is listed Grade II*.</p>{%- endset -%}
{%- set churchAbout -%}<h2>The church through the centuries</h2>
    <p>The chancel is 14th century, and so is the timber roof of the north transept. The tower was built in the 15th century, with battlements, pinnacles at the corners and gargoyles beneath them. Much of the nave is from a restoration of 1844.</p>
    <h2>Things to see</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A marble lectern made in the 12th century for Evesham Abbey. Dug up in the abbey churchyard in 1813, it has stood in St Egwin's since 1865.</li>
      <li>The Bigge family monuments in the north transept: Thomas Bigge, who died in 1581, lies beside his wife; Sir Thomas Bigge (died 1613) and his wife kneel beneath an arch; and Sir Thomas Bigge (died 1621) lies in alabaster under a canopy held up by black marble columns, with his helmet, gauntlets and spurs hanging over the tombs.</li>
      <li>The octagonal 15th-century font, the pulpit made from 17th-century panelling, and linenfold panels in the chancel.</li>
    </ul>{%- endset -%}
{%- set servicesIntro -%}<p>St Egwin's has a service on many Sundays, taking turns with the other churches. Coming up:</p>{%- endset -%}
